{"title": "Lecture 3 – Thyroid function tests", "html": "<p class=\"muted small\">Source: CursIII-MGE introducere 2023.pptx – 10 slides</p><div class=\"slide\"><h3>THE THYROID GLAND <span class=\"sn\">#1</span></h3></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>I <span class=\"sn\">#2</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\">Thyroid Function Tests and other investigations</li><li class=\"l0\">Disorders</li><li class=\"l0\">Goiter</li><li class=\"l0\">Thyroid nodule and thyroid cancer</li><li class=\"l0\">Thyroiditis</li><li class=\"l0\">II</li><li class=\"l0\">Hyperthyroidism / Graves&#x27; Disease / Toxic Nodular Goiter /Hypothyroidism</li><li class=\"l0\">Topics</li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Thyroid function regulation <span class=\"sn\">#3</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>TRH</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>TSH</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>thyroid</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>FT4, FT3</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>T3, T4</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>(-)</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>(-)</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>(-)</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>(+)</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>(+)</b></li><li class=\"l0\">Tissues</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>T3←T4</b></li><li class=\"l0\">The levels of thyroid hormone in the blood are tightly controlled by feedback mechanisms involved in the hypothalamo–pituitary–thyroid (HPT) axis (see Fig.) (and, as elsewhere discussed, by iodide through the elements of autoregulation)</li><li class=\"l0\">TRH stimulates the synthesis and secretion of TSH.</li><li class=\"l0\">• TSH secreted by the pituitary stimulates the thyroid to secrete principally T4 and also T3.</li><li class=\"l0\">T4 and T3 are bound to TBG, Transthyretin, and albumin. The remaining free hormones inhibit the synthesis and release of TRH and TSH (only 0.04% of T4 and 0.4% of T3 are unbound or free)</li><li class=\"l0\">•T4 is converted peripherally to the metabolically active T3 or the inactive rT3. About 80% of T4 is metabolized by deiodination, 35% to T3 and 45% to rT3</li><li class=\"l0\">(+)</li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Tests of the Thyroid Function <span class=\"sn\">#4</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>I. Basic tests</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>TSH,FT4, FT3, TT3,TT4 → as measuring indicators to assess thyroid function</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>TSH - immunometric assays (IMA) To measure TSH in a blood sample= the best way to initially test thyroid function (because thyroid dysfunction usually arises from primary disorders of the thyroid gland, serum TSH measurement is the most widely employed test to determine whether thyroid dysfunction exists)</b></li><li class=\"l0\">= as an “early warning system” – often occurring before the actual level of thyroid hormones in the body becomes too high or too low.</li><li class=\"l0\">↑TSH = primary hypothyroidism</li><li class=\"l0\">↓TSH, usually hyperthyroidism</li><li class=\"l0\">!!!The test may be misleading in patients with secondary thyroid dysfunction due to hypothalamic/pituitary disease</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>T4 TESTS A Total T4 measures the bound and free hormone and can change when binding proteins differ.</b></li><li class=\"l0\">A Free T4 measures what is not bound and able to enter and affect the body tissues.</li><li class=\"l0\">Tests measuring free T4 – either a free T4 (FT4) or free T4 index (FTI) – more accurately reflect how the thyroid gland is functioning when checked with a TSH.</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>Interpretation of tests</b></li><li class=\"l0\">TSH&lt;0,1 µU/ml + ↑FT4 = hyperthyroidism</li><li class=\"l0\">TSH –N/↑ +↑FT4= TSH = secreting pituitary tumor</li><li class=\"l0\">↓ FT4 + TSH&gt;10 µU/ml = primary hypothyroidism (overt primary hypothyroidism)</li><li class=\"l0\">↓ FT4 + ↓TSH = central hypothyroidism</li><li class=\"l0\">T4(thyroxine)= the major thyroid hormone secreted by the thyroid gland</li><li class=\"l0\">T3 (triiodothyronine)← T4 in the liver and in certain tissues where T3 acts</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>⇒free T4 is the more important hormone to measure.</b></li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Slide 5 <span class=\"sn\">#5</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>T3 TESTST3 tests may be useful to diagnose hyperthyroidism or to determine the severity of the hyperthyroidism</b></li></ul><figure><img data-src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"slide 5\"></figure></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>II. Dynamic tests <span class=\"sn\">#6</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>The TRH stimulation test → which can be used to assess the functional state of the TSH secretory mechanism (to assess the function of thyrotrophic cells →in diagnosis of central hypothyroidism, see chapter Hypopituitarism)</b></li><li class=\"l0\">check TSH at 0’ than give protirelin (TRH) 200-400 µg iv and check TSH 60 minutes later; a normal response: TSH at 60’ increases 2x the basal values.</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>It is now of limited use;</b></li><li class=\"l0\"><b>its main use is in the differential diagnosis of elevated TSH in the setting of ↑FT4/ in the differential diagnosis of resistance to thyroid hormone and a TSH-secreting pituitary adenoma</b></li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Antibody screen <span class=\"sn\">#7</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\">High titers of antithyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO) antibodies and/or antithyroglobulin antibodies are found in patients with autoimmune thyroid disease</li><li class=\"l0\">Hashimoto’s thyroiditis,</li><li class=\"l0\">Graves’s disease, and</li><li class=\"l0\">sometimes euthyroid individuals</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>Thyroid ultrasonography</b></li><li class=\"l0\">The standard transducer for neck imaging is a multi-frequency, linear array probe in the 10–15 MHz range which is ideal for examining the superficial structures of the neck</li><li class=\"l0\">Provides an accurate indication of thyroid size ((the thyroid volume-upper normal limit  &lt; 16ml in women and &lt;18ml in men) and</li><li class=\"l0\">it can reveal the structure of the thyroid gland: an enlarged/ or not, hypoechoic gland suggests either Graves’disease or subacute thyroiditis, Hashimoto’s thyroiditis.</li><li class=\"l0\">It  is useful for differentiating cystic nodules from solid ones;</li><li class=\"l0\">but it cannot be used to distinguish between benign and malignant disease.</li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Radionuclide Imaging and Radionuclide uptake <span class=\"sn\">#8</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\">The underlying principle is that isotopes that are selectively accumulated by thyroid tissue can be detected by a gamma camera and the data transformed into a visual display.</li><li class=\"l0\">123I and technetium 99mTc pertechnetate (99mTcO4) are useful for determining the functional activity and morphology of the thyroid gland.</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>Scintiscanning (a thyroid scan→ “a picture” of the thyroid gland)</b></li><li class=\"l0\">It provides information  about the size and shape of the thyroid gland and the distribution of tracer activity  within the gland →  which occasionally helps in cases of uncertainty as to the cause of the thyrotoxicosis (see Table).</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>The main indications:</b></li><li class=\"l0\">• To assess if a thyroid nodule is „hot” (functioning)  or „cold”</li><li class=\"l0\">• To distinguish between Graves’s disease and a thyroiditis (autoimmune or viral—de Quervain’s thyroiditis).</li><li class=\"l0\">• To detect retrosternal goitre.</li><li class=\"l0\">• To detect ectopic thyroid tissue.</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>Radionuclide uptake  ( 123I or Tc) can be measured in conjunction with imaging.</b></li><li class=\"l0\">By measuring the amount of radioactivity that is taken up by the thyroid gland (radioactive iodine uptake, RAIU) →whether the gland is functioning normally.</li><li class=\"l0\">RAIU by the thyroid varies with recent dietary iodide intake and with countries.</li><li class=\"l0\">\tRAIU (24 h) - directly proportionate with TSH levels</li><li class=\"l0\">- indirectly proportionate with iodine pool)</li><li class=\"l0\">A very high (↑) RAIU is seen in individuals whose thyroid gland is  overactive (hyperthyroidism) or with goiters from regions with iodine deficiency (iodine-avid thyroids)  while</li><li class=\"l0\">a low (↓) RAIU is seen when the thyroid gland is underactive (hypothyroidism).</li><li class=\"l0\"><b>!!! RAIU does not determine the hormonal functional status of thyroid gland, even though it can be useful to differentiate among the causes of thyrotoxicosis</b></li><li class=\"l0\">FT4 and TSH ( +T3) measurements reflect THYROID FUNCTION</li></ul></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Slide 9 <span class=\"sn\">#9</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>Thyroid Biopsy</b></li><li class=\"l0\">Fine-needle aspiration biopsy (FNAB)/cytology of a thyroid nodule is the best method for differentiating benign from malignant thyroid nodules and diffuse goiters.</li></ul><table class=\"ct\"><tr><td>Radionuclide scanning (scintigram) in thyroid disease</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Condition</td><td>Scan appearance</td></tr><tr><td>Graves’s hyperthyroidism\n \nThyroiditis (e.g. de Quervain’s)\nToxic nodule\nThyrotoxicosis factitia\nToxic multinodular goiter</td><td>Enlarged gland with homogeneous radionucleotide uptake\n\nLow or absent uptake\nA solitary area of high uptake\nDepressed thyroid uptake\nMultiple functioning nodules (areas of high uptake) in the gland or occasionally an irregular, patchy distribution of radioactive iodine</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"slide\"><h3>Slide 10 <span class=\"sn\">#10</span></h3><ul class=\"sl\"><li class=\"l0\"><b>Ilie Ioana Rada. Introduction to Endocrinology. Springer Nature Switzerland AG, 2020</b></li><li class=\"l0\">https://www.springer.com/in/book/9783030273811</li><li class=\"l0\">&quot;Diagnostic tests and algorithms in Endocrinology” Ioana Popa-Ilie &amp;Carmen Georgescu, Ed.Med.Univ. Cluj-Napoca, 2022</li><li class=\"l0\">Oxford Handbook of Endocrinology and Diabetes, John Wass &amp; Katharine Owen, 3rd Edition, Oxford University Press, 2014</li><li class=\"l0\">Bibliography</li><li class=\"l0\">Bibliography</li></ul></div>"}